# 🔟 MATRIX MULTIPLICATION ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

Now we scale this idea to many samples.

Suppose:

$$
X=
\begin{bmatrix}
1&2\\
3&4
\end{bmatrix}
$$

and:

$$
W=
\begin{bmatrix}
5\\
6
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Then:

$$
XW=
\begin{bmatrix}
1(5)+2(6)\\
3(5)+4(6)
\end{bmatrix}
$$

$$
=
\begin{bmatrix}
17\\
39
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Python:

```python
X = np.array([
    [1, 2],
    [3, 4]
])

W = np.array([
    [5],
    [6]
])

print(X @ W)
```

Output:

```text
[[17]
 [39]]
```

---

# 🧠 THE MOST IMPORTANT MATRIX RULE

If:

```text
A = (m × n)
B = (n × p)
```

Then:

```text
A @ B = (m × p)
```

The **inner dimensions must match**.

Example:

```text
(3 × 4) @ (4 × 2)
```

Valid.

Result:

```text
(3 × 2)
```

But:

```text
(3 × 4) @ (3 × 2)
```

❌ Invalid.

Because:

```text
4 ≠ 3
```

---

# 🎯 INTERVIEW TRICK

Remember:

> **Inside must match, outside becomes the answer.**

```text
(3 × 4) @ (4 × 2)
  ↑          ↑
  match!

Result:

(3 × 2)
 ↑     ↑
outside dimensions
```

